  "question": "What owns table topology, cell coordinates, selection, clipboard and layout?",
  "model": "GPT-6 / Codex (fresh model-selected revalidation)",
  "method": "Best API Review; Redesign from First Principles; source and consumer reinspection; adversarial delivery-contract review",
  "trigger": "Re-run the final table direction exercise after the user changed the model, without treating the prior pursue verdict as authoritative.",
  "requirements": [
    "Preserve held native ranges, merged-span closure, exact sparse cells, rows/tables and mixed-table paint under one canonical selection.",
    "Keep table topology and paint projection in Plate while Plite owns canonical selection, view/root scoping and mounted DOM lifetime.",
    "Do not silently change the public React delivery semantics of render.useViewElementAttributes or require custom raw hosts to migrate without an explicit contract decision.",
    "Accept painter deletion only after an exact production-shaped, long-lived comparison passes without forced garbage collection and native lifecycle proof remains reachable."
  ],
  "evidenceReuse": "Reused the source-identified 25-test semantic/host prototype, controlled four-cohort receipt and preserved uncontrolled receipt because the decision-critical renderer, store, table painter/projection and Plite DOM runtime fingerprints remain unchanged. Reinspected the public callback types, API docs and all first-party producers. Current Plite public-state and browser table-selection inputs changed after the prior record; their current selection/root and native table contracts were reread and do not resolve host delivery.",
  "summary": "Retain Plate table semantics, the single-cell-layer target and the rejection of generic Plite table markers. Stop the scalar delivery design as written and defer painter deletion. The proposed binder silently splits one public callback: data/aria stop reaching custom React element props while class/style/placeholder still do. Its controlled action benchmark passes only with symmetric forced collection outside timing, while the uncontrolled stress cohort fails 87.56ms versus 46.59ms. Compare the repaired painter, a table-specific binder and an explicit host-only channel before selecting generic machinery or deleting anything.",
  "verdict": "defer",
  "previous": "2026-09-17-table-host-projection-final-design",
  "relation": "supersedes",
  "alternatives": [
    "Generic Plite selected-cell or anchor markers: reject; table span closure, held ranges and row/table projection are product semantics.",
    "Keep and locally repair the current table painter: valid production baseline; it preserves the public render contract while its same-key replacement and cache lifetime defects are repaired.",
    "Table-specific host binding: preferred least-machinery challenger; it must prove custom table cells can participate without another public hook or duplicated lifetime registry.",
    "Silently split render.useViewElementAttributes by attribute name: stop; custom component props and render behavior change without an expressed API boundary.",
    "Add a distinct scalar-only host-attribute source while preserving useViewElementAttributes: honest generic candidate, but one table optimization has not yet earned the public concept and production scale is unproved.",
    "Redefine the whole existing callback as host-only: possible breaking target only if class/style/placeholder and all custom-host migrations are solved coherently."
  ],
  "proofLimits": "Read-only design revalidation; no product implementation or native/browser run. Core delivery and table projection sources match the prior receipt. The 25 artifact tests and controlled p95 cohorts pass, but collection is forced outside the timed lifecycle. The preserved uncontrolled stress cohort fails 87.56ms candidate versus 46.59ms baseline. No production renderer integration, long-lived heap/allocation result, SSR/hydration, native held-drag/caret/paint or downstream custom-host migration proof exists.",
